==English==

===Etymology===
From the [[Latin]] name for [[Melbourne]], ''Melburnia'', {{suffix|en||ian}}.

The term can be traced back to 1876, when the case for ''Melburnian'' over ''Melbournian'' was made in the {{w|Melbourne Grammar School}} publication, the ''Melburnian''. 'The {{w|diphthong}}, ‘ou’ is not a Latin diphthong: hence, we argued this way, ''Melburnia'' would be [the] Latin form of name, and from it comes ''Melburnian''.' <ref>See ''Oxford English Dictionary Additions Series'', iii, s.v. '[https://books.google.com.au/books?id=gUGcAQAAQBAJ&pg=PA291&dq=oed+melburnian&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0CB0Q6AEwAGoVChMIrtWm4sr0xwIVxyKmCh1yUAPF#v=onepage&q=melburnian&f=false Melburnian]'. Accessed 13 September 2015.</ref>

